## OCB-CAL-01 — calibrated confusion and destination-dependent censoring

Let `p_i` be the true destination distribution from prepared visible state `i`.
Let `eta_j>0` be the calibrated probability that a jump to true destination `j`
is retained, and let `C[j,y]` be a calibrated confusion matrix from true
destination `j` to reported label `y`.

Conditioned on observing a recorded jump,

`q_i = C^T diag(eta) p_i / (eta^T p_i)`.

If `C` is invertible and every `eta_j>0`, define

`B = diag(eta)^(-1) (C^T)^(-1)`.

Then

`B q_i = p_i/(eta^T p_i)`.

Thus every ordinal comparison is linear in the observed probability vector:

`sign(p_i[a]-p_i[b]) = sign((e_a-e_b)^T B q_i)`.

No nonlinear renormalization is needed to certify the top-two set.

If `||qhat-q||_inf<=epsilon`, comparison `a>b` is certified whenever

`(e_a-e_b)^T B qhat > epsilon ||(e_a-e_b)^T B||_1`.

### Synthetic stress fixture

For a nontrivial test only (not apparatus data), use:

- 90 percent correct reported destination;
- 5 percent confusion to each cyclic adjacent label;
- destination efficiencies ranging from 0.80 to 0.98.

Exact inversion recovers the strict law to `1.7e-16`.
The worst strict nearest-neighbour-versus-competitor inequality survives any
simultaneous observed-coordinate error below

`epsilon = 0.061363680539136226`.

A direct Hoeffding/union bound over all `12x12` reported frequencies gives
1,150 recorded exits per prepared state for a 95 percent family-wise synthetic
certificate.  This is conservative and is not a laboratory sample-size claim.

### Failure controls

- singular `C`: carrier can be erased completely (uniform confusion has rank 1);
- unknown `eta`: only the normalized products `eta_j p_j` are identified;
- any `eta_j=0`: that destination is invisible without extra assumptions.

So calibration is not bookkeeping; it is part of the operational bridge.

## OCB-CHANNEL-01 — first-nonscalar-jet carrier theorem

A common analytic functional calculus is not enough to guarantee a common
carrier.

Suppose near a declared control origin

`K_epsilon = c(epsilon) I - a epsilon^m A + R_epsilon`,

with `a>0`, `m>=1`, and destination score monotone in `|K_ij|` in the relevant
small-response regime.  If

`max_(i!=j) |R_ij| < (a epsilon^m/2)(w_1-w_2)`,

then for every prepared vertex the two largest off-diagonal destination scores
are exactly the two strict distance-one neighbours.  Replacing `w_1-w_2` by
the smallest adjacent shell gap gives full six-shell ordering.

This is the shared structure behind the previously certified heat, unitary and
wave short-control carrier windows: their first non-scalar jet is proportional
to `A`.

### Counterexample: functional calculus alone

Let `P_1` be the spectral projector onto the `k=1` Fourier doublet.  Because the
seven sector eigenvalues are distinct, `P_1=f(A)` for a polynomial of degree at
most six.  Nevertheless its shell amplitudes are

`(0.1443376, 0.0833333, 0, -0.0833333, -0.1443376, -0.1666667)`,

so the largest absolute response is at the antipode `d=6`, not `d=1`.
The projector, commutator and polynomial reconstruction residuals are all at
floating replay level (`<=1.5e-13`).

Therefore:

> Carrier robustness is a first-jet statement, not a generic `K=f(A)` statement.

This does not select heat, unitary, wave, or any physical state category.
